    "product_id": "cd10a3b1-5d65-435c-abe7-53b28020242d",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 080816\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n115 AM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Elevated flows will continue on the Salt and Gila rivers through \nat least the middle of the week due to dam releases. \n\n- Dry weather with occasional breeziness and temperatures near to \nslightly above the daily normals will be common through much of the \nweek. \n\n- Unsettled weather with increased chances of showers may return to \nthe region over the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Tuesday/...\nEarly morning WV imagery shows a compact, cyclonic circulation \npropagating south through Sonora while high pressure ridging builds \ninto the SW Conus. In the northern stream, a progressive wave was \nbreaking onshore over the Pacific NW/SW Canada aiding in the \nintensification and inland push of the aforementioned high pressure \nridge to the south. This configuration will support large scale \nsubsidence and surface pressure rises across the region while \ninnocuous, low amplitude features settle over northern Mexico in the \nnext 72 hours. The enhanced pressure gradient will support some \ngusty morning east winds across ridge tops and terrain gaps in south-\ncentral Arizona the next couple days. This is a very typical weather \npattern for the season, and forecast wind speeds do not appear \nparticularly unusual. Otherwise, H5 heights will hover in a 580-\n585dm range which is slightly above average for early/mid November. \nEnsemble numerical spread remains quite narrow resulting in \nexcellent confidence of high temperatures 2F-5F above normal with \nmorning lows slightly more anomalous. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Wednesday through Saturday/...\nAn unusually early season sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) event \nwill be the primary influence towards hemispheric pattern \namplification this week featuring ridge retrogression poleward and \ndeep negative height anomalies descending into the NE Conus and East \nPacific. During the initial stages of this transition, strong \nsubsident high pressure with nearly steady midtropospheric heights \nwill be situated directly over Arizona yielding a high confidence \nforecast of a very tranquil, near persistence temperatures a couple \ndegrees above normal. Late in the week, there is growing ensemble \nagreement that shortwave energy will detach from the larger East \nPacific trough and progress towards the SW Conus/northern Mexico. \nHowever, significant spread persists regarding the timing of \nincoming negative height anomalies and jet energy that would \ninfluence regional weather. Given the evolution of this highly \namplified and semi-blocked pattern, would generally prefer a \nsolution towards the slower end of the ensemble envelope bringing \nheightened rainfall chances into the CWA Saturday/Sunday. \nRegardless, run-to-run variations are still likely the next several \ndays with respect to details in timing and location of the best \nforcing resulting in limited forecast confidence.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0500Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo aviation weather concerns under mostly clear skies can be \nexpected throughout the TAF period. Outside of periods of VRB \nconditions, E'rly winds will prevail through Sunday evening, with \nperhaps a few gusts in the mid-teens being observed during the late \nmorning hours. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours under \nclear skies. Outside of extended periods of VRB conditions, winds at \nKBLH will switch between NW and NE, while KIPL experiences generally \nNW/W'rly flow.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions will prevail across the region through the majority \nof the week with some enhanced morning ridge top and terrain gap \nbreeziness the next couple days. Minimum afternoon humidity levels \nwill primarily fall into a 20-30% range in western districts and 30-\n50% in eastern districts with a tendency for drying towards the \nmiddle/end of the week. This will follow fair to good overnight \nrecovery in western districts and good to excellent levels in \neastern districts. Outside of the early week breeziness, wind speeds \nwill be light with directional tendencies towards a northerly or \neasterly component. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18\nAVIATION...RW\nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
